A||||12||danny boy||Sway bar sizes/positions.||||1||
Z||000000||danny boy||03-29-2001||12:51 AM|||| Well, I've tried the 1" swaybar in front and 3/4" on the rear and didn't like it. I took the Front bar off and put the stock bar back on with all urethane.Better, but still pushes a tad too much for my tastes.This was all done on an '86 base coupe that I recently got rid of. Now that I've got the GT, I'm gonna try a stock bar on front and 1" on rear. Has anyone done this? <BR>I have coilovers on the rear and drop springs on the front if that helps.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Will||03-29-2001||03:27 PM||||On my '87 I have WS6 bars at both ends. I like it a lot. It has just a hint of oversteer, and rolls very little since I installed the rod end sway bar links.<P>The only other mod I have is aluminum cradle bushings. Spring rates affect swaybar sizes; if you are using non-stock spring rates, then the swaybar sizes you need to achieve the same balance will be different.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||03-29-2001||03:38 PM||||stock front bar and bushings.. big rear bar with poly.. Oversteer MONSTER!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and aluminum cradle bushings. poly rear bushigns are cheaper and it'll help just as much. i love it!... same set up on the Miata. mmm... drifting..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||03-29-2001||04:37 PM||||85SE v-6 4spd<BR>Stock front bar<BR>J.C Whitney Addco bar on rear.<P>Love it !!<P>Very near neutral steer and can push to oversteer when needed via throttle.  Very flat conering.<P>Most cost effective upgrade for handling.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||03-29-2001||05:03 PM||||I have tried 3 different sets of lowering springs , 4 different setups of sway bars , incl a smaller than stock front , 2 sway bars on the rear (1 from a z51 vette) 1 set of rear springs was a set of units made by eibach for the front of an impala SS , Fiero store front , lowering springs , Eibach lowering springs , Stock springs cut 1 coil. All of these different setups run with street rubber and R compound tires, over a 5 year period , I have an attic full of parts<P>This is what I have found to be the best.<P>Ground control coil overs in the rear , these have been modified to fit snuggly over the Koni rear struts , set at full hard. The coil over springs are 340Lbs rating , a bit stiff , but good. I have a Fiero store bar out back , I forget the dimensions, The front uses a set of Carrera springs and koni shocks set 2 clicks from full soft , the front bar is a heavy duty unit , I will measure ASAP and get back to you. Moog problem solver slotted ball joints , 1.6 Degrees of Negative camber , I run Pirelli P zero's 255/40/17's out back , 225/45's up front , The car has a bit of rake to it , (The front is a bit lower) <P>Basically we set up the car the same as our 94 911 RSR that we use for Racing , hard in the back soft in the front. This is the only way to get the front to work , I was told this by a very successfull fiero racer Andy bettencourt.<P>How it handles..Nothing like a Fiero!!!<P>No trailing throttle oversteer , NO Understeer with street tires! I was getting fed up after 5 years of autocrossing and tracking the car. IF you drove it 10-10ths on the track it was scary because it had the potential to bite you.. Now it drifts nicely when needed, no squirely handling characteristics (Ever done a donut on the street?) A bit scary.. Now I can plant it on every apex perfectly! Give it gas out an off ramp , it doesn't push it digs in , Same as coming onto an offramp , lift if you get going into hot and it just corners...<P>The car is finally fun to drive and extremely predictable. Only change this year is a set of new rear Pirelli's and my 11.25" discs...<P>You may want to try the Fiero raicng list , lots of autocrossers/ track racers , 170 in total last I checked. <P>it's located on yahoogroups<P>Jonathan McCreery<P> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||03-29-2001||05:45 PM||||Slammed:<P>What front rates are you running?||||reg||1
Z||000006||hugh||03-29-2001||08:09 PM||||Johnathan,I'm in need of a rear sway bar for my 88GT.I have toronado transaxle with a 350 chevy.I would like to put on a bigger bar than is currently available for the Fiero.You mention a bar from a corvette,Is it bigger than the heavy duty bar for Fiero's?Was it much work to mount,does it use the stock end link mounts?If it can work,is it for sale?  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||danny boy||03-29-2001||09:12 PM||||I have the held motorsports coilovers out back with 300# springs and some of those"we get em in a 55 gal. drum" mystery springs the Fiero Store used to sell.<BR>The stock bar is on front with URETHANE and the rear bar(will) have RUBBER. I had the Fiero Store rear bar and didn't like it. the rear bar I'm using is the matching Front bar.<BR>   1FST, the aluminum was free and so is the machine work.Its all who ya know and how bored they are...<P> Oh, I also am having 1"spacers made to raise the front up and still have better than stock spring rate.I'm tired of dragging on fill covers at gas stations.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
